USTAT(2)                       P32W Unix                        USTAT(2)

NAME
       ustat - space on a mounted filesystem

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/ustat.h>

       int ustat(int dev, struct ustat *buf);

DESCRIPTION
       ustat fills buf with the kernel's counts for the filesystem
       mounted from the block device numbered dev (the st_rdev that
       stat(2) gives for the device's special file, such as
       /dev/disk or /dev/ram):

              struct ustat {
                  unsigned f_blocks, f_bfree;   /* 1 KiB blocks */
                  unsigned f_files, f_ffree;    /* inodes */
                  unsigned f_clean;             /* clean at the mount */
              };

       f_blocks and f_files are the filesystem's whole size, f_bfree
       and f_ffree what is free now. f_clean is 1 if the filesystem
       had been shut down cleanly when it was mounted (fsck -c uses
       it). The counts are the kernel's
       own, so they are current without a sync(2), and any user may
       ask. df(1) uses it; mkfs(8) and fsck(8) use it to find out
       whether a device is mounted.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success. -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EINVAL  No filesystem is mounted from dev.

       EFAULT  buf is not a valid address.

EXAMPLES
       struct stat st;
       struct ustat u;
       if (stat("/dev/ram", &st) == 0 && ustat(st.st_rdev, &u) == 0)
           printf("%u of %u blocks free\n", u.f_bfree, u.f_blocks);

SEE ALSO
       df(1), stat(2), mount(2), fs(5)

BUGS
       It is system call 74, P32W Unix's own: V7's ustat had a
       different structure (free blocks, free inodes and the
       filesystem's name).
